  "result": "Done. All four tests pass, and the implementation lives in `editor/buffer.py`.\n\n**Approach chosen:** A, the command pattern. Each edit is stored as a small record of its kind, position, and the affected text. Insert and delete over the same range are exact inverses, so one record serves both undo and redo. I picked it because the spec requires unbounded history, and snapshotting the whole buffer per edit (Approach B) would make memory grow with buffer size times history length. With commands, memory only grows with the size of the edits themselves.\n\nOther decisions I made without asking:\n- **Negative delete length** raises IndexError, alongside the out-of-range checks the spec calls for.\n- **Any new edit** clears the redo stack, per the spec.\n- No git commit was made, since you didn't ask for one.",
